1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,866
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ocean absorbs a large share of humanity's
carbon emissions,</font>

2
00:00:04,946 --> 00:00:10,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet our official picture of how much it takes up
always lags reality by about a year.</font>

3
00:00:10,945 --> 00:00:16,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work introduces Carbon Monitor Ocean, or
CMO-NRT, a near-real-time, monthly,</font>

4
00:00:16,662 --> 00:00:22,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">gridded dataset of global surface ocean fugacity of
CO2 and air-sea CO2 flux.</font>

5
00:00:22,097 --> 00:00:28,439
<font color="#FFFFFF">By using convolutional neural networks and
semi-supervised learning to update the estimates</font>

6
00:00:28,519 --> 00:00:32,745
<font color="#FFFFFF">from ten ocean biogeochemical models and eight data
products,</font>

7
00:00:32,825 --> 00:00:37,332
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors extend the global carbon budget to the
present month,</font>

8
00:00:37,412 --> 00:00:43,190
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving scientists and policymakers a far more
timely view of the ocean carbon sink.</font>

9
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:47,776
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ocean is one of the largest buffers against
climate change,</font>

10
00:00:47,856 --> 00:00:51,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">soaking up a big fraction of the carbon dioxide we emit.</font>

11
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:55,394
<font color="#FFFFFF">But our best official accounting of how much it absorbs,</font>

12
00:00:55,474 --> 00:01:00,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">the annual Global Carbon Budget, always trails
reality by about a year.</font>

13
00:01:00,304 --> 00:01:03,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">That means when the ocean's uptake shifts,</font>

14
00:01:03,161 --> 00:01:06,482
<font color="#FFFFFF">we simply cannot see it until long after the fact.</font>

15
00:01:06,562 --> 00:01:11,516
<font color="#FFFFFF">This lag is baked in: the biogeochemical models are
computationally heavy,</font>

16
00:01:11,596 --> 00:01:17,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the surface-ocean observations they rely on are
themselves delayed by roughly a year.</font>

17
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:22,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">As countries move through the global stocktake
under the Paris Agreement,</font>

18
00:01:22,515 --> 00:01:26,813
<font color="#FFFFFF">they need to know where the carbon is going right
now, not a year ago.</font>

19
00:01:26,893 --> 00:01:31,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">The existing databases of ocean carbon fluxes are
detailed and trustworthy,</font>

20
00:01:31,584 --> 00:01:34,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they are always looking backward in time.</font>

21
00:01:34,398 --> 00:01:38,946
<font color="#FFFFFF">What is missing is a way to bring that same rigor
up to the present month,</font>

22
00:01:39,026 --> 00:01:44,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">so that policymakers and scientists can respond to
changes in the ocean sink as they happen.</font>

23
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:51,006
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core contribution is Carbon Monitor Ocean, or
CMO-NRT: a near-real-time, monthly,</font>

24
00:01:51,086 --> 00:01:56,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">gridded dataset of global surface ocean carbon
dioxide fugacity and air-sea flux,</font>

25
00:01:56,810 --> 00:02:01,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">together with the machine-learning framework that
generates it.</font>

26
00:02:01,261 --> 00:02:04,643
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rather than replace the trusted community models,</font>

27
00:02:04,723 --> 00:02:10,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">it updates all ten ocean biogeochemical models and
eight data products from the Global</font>

28
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:14,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">Carbon Budget 2022 into a near-real-time framework.</font>

29
00:02:14,404 --> 00:02:20,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">The dataset and the code are released openly, on
Figshare and on the project website.</font>

30
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:26,294
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method treats each biogeochemical model or data
product as a target that a neural</font>

31
00:02:26,374 --> 00:02:30,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">network learns to reproduce from observable
predictors: year,</font>

32
00:02:30,439 --> 00:02:32,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">month, latitude, longitude,</font>

33
00:02:32,238 --> 00:02:36,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">and nine environmental variables like sea-surface
temperature, salinity,</font>

34
00:02:37,036 --> 00:02:38,356
<font color="#FFFFFF">chlorophyll and wind.</font>

35
00:02:38,436 --> 00:02:46,152
<font color="#FFFFFF">Global grids are cut into small
eighteen-by-eighteen patches and fed through stacked convolutional and linear layers.</font>

36
00:02:46,232 --> 00:02:52,883
<font color="#FFFFFF">The clever part is semi-supervised learning:
alongside a standard supervised error on labeled points,</font>

37
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:58,814
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model adds an unsupervised consistency loss
that forces its predictions to agree when</font>

38
00:02:58,894 --> 00:03:03,278
<font color="#FFFFFF">ten percent versus thirty percent of the input
features are masked.</font>

39
00:03:03,358 --> 00:03:07,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">Combining the two losses makes the model markedly
more stable.</font>

40
00:03:07,790 --> 00:03:10,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">The resulting dataset delivers monthly,</font>

41
00:03:10,636 --> 00:03:16,249
<font color="#FFFFFF">gridded maps of global surface-ocean carbon dioxide
fugacity and air-sea flux,</font>

42
00:03:16,329 --> 00:03:19,168
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning January 2022 through July 2023,</font>

43
00:03:19,248 --> 00:03:27,268
<font color="#FFFFFF">built by updating the ten ocean biogeochemical
models and eight data products of the Global Carbon Budget 2022.</font>

44
00:03:27,348 --> 00:03:33,253
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its inputs draw on satellite and reanalysis
products for chlorophyll, temperature,</font>

45
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:38,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">sea ice, mixed-layer depth, salinity, sea-surface
height, pressure and wind,</font>

46
00:03:38,879 --> 00:03:45,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the whole dataset is published openly on
Figshare and refreshed on the project website.</font>

47
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:47,112
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test the approach,</font>

48
00:03:47,192 --> 00:03:51,947
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors held out the most recent two years of
every model and product,</font>

49
00:03:52,027 --> 00:03:56,455
<font color="#FFFFFF">trained only on earlier data, and then predicted
the withheld period.</font>

50
00:03:56,535 --> 00:04:02,924
<font color="#FFFFFF">The predictions track the originals remarkably
well: for most of the ten models and eight products,</font>

51
00:04:03,004 --> 00:04:06,256
<font color="#FFFFFF">the correlation exceeds an R-squared of point nine,</font>

52
00:04:06,336 --> 00:04:11,091
<font color="#FFFFFF">and even the global monthly aggregates mostly stay
above point eight five.</font>

53
00:04:11,171 --> 00:04:14,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">On global averages the predictions run just slightly high,</font>

54
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:18,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">with most differences smaller than three microatmospheres.</font>

55
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:24,702
<font color="#FFFFFF">Looking model by model reveals a clear pattern: the
biogeochemical models are reproduced</font>

56
00:04:24,782 --> 00:04:26,851
<font color="#FFFFFF">most faithfully and consistently,</font>

57
00:04:26,931 --> 00:04:30,434
<font color="#FFFFFF">with several exceeding an R-squared of point nine five,</font>

58
00:04:30,514 --> 00:04:36,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">while a few data products are noisier and
occasionally scatter away from the fit line.</font>

59
00:04:36,115 --> 00:04:41,963
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spatial maps of the differences confirm that
agreement is broadly uniform across the globe,</font>

60
00:04:42,043 --> 00:04:47,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the largest residual gaps concentrated in the
Arctic Ocean and the equatorial Pacific.</font>

61
00:04:48,270 --> 00:04:50,491
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

62
00:04:50,571 --> 00:04:55,651
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method collapses the roughly one-year reporting
delay down to monthly,</font>

63
00:04:55,731 --> 00:05:00,532
<font color="#FFFFFF">near-real-time coverage, here spanning January 2022
through July 2023.</font>

64
00:05:00,612 --> 00:05:07,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">Validation correlations exceed an R-squared of
point nine for most of the eighteen source estimates,</font>

65
00:05:07,584 --> 00:05:10,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">global aggregates stay above point eight five,</font>

66
00:05:10,792 --> 00:05:15,663
<font color="#FFFFFF">and global monthly differences are mostly under
three microatmospheres.</font>

67
00:05:15,743 --> 00:05:22,217
<font color="#FFFFFF">Even the auxiliary near-real-time atmospheric
carbon dioxide model achieves a root-mean-square</font>

68
00:05:22,297 --> 00:05:24,657
<font color="#FFFFFF">error of just one point seven four,</font>

69
00:05:24,737 --> 00:05:26,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">about a half-percent error.</font>

70
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:32,453
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple: by pairing convolutional
networks with semi-supervised learning</font>

71
00:05:32,533 --> 00:05:35,227
<font color="#FFFFFF">to update trusted models and data products,</font>

72
00:05:35,307 --> 00:05:42,258
<font color="#FFFFFF">Carbon Monitor Ocean turns a once year-delayed
picture of the global ocean carbon sink into a near-real-time,</font>

73
00:05:42,338 --> 00:05:43,871
<font color="#FFFFFF">monthly, gridded monitor.</font>

74
00:05:43,951 --> 00:05:49,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">That gives scientists and policymakers a far
timelier and spatially detailed constraint</font>

75
00:05:49,564 --> 00:05:52,193
<font color="#FFFFFF">on how much carbon the ocean is taking up,</font>

76
00:05:52,273 --> 00:05:56,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">right when they need it for the carbon budget and
climate decisions.</font>
